window.docContents[102].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Lie-algebras-defined-by-ad-relations/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.16. Problems on Lie algebras\u0009Lie algebras defined by ad relations\u0009Lie algebras defined by ad relations","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Lie-algebras-defined-by-ad-relations/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___3___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Lie-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Lie-algebra--Lie-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary type depending on a commutative ring and a Lie ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.9.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Lie algebra\u0009Lie algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Lie-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Lie-algebra--Lie-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Section-5___12___-Representations-of-S_n/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___12___heading-1":{"contents":"In this subsection we give a description of the representations of the symmetric group S_n for any n.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.12. Representations of $S_n$\u0009Section 5.12: Representations of S_n\u0009Section 5.12: Representations of S_n","header":"5.12. Representations of  S_n","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Section-5___12___-Representations-of-S_n/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___12___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/#chapter-05___section-5-18":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/#chapter-05___section-5-18"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span/#Chapter5___Theorem5___26___1":{"contents":"Theorem 5.26.1. Let X be a conjugation-invariant system of subgroups of a finite group G. Then two conditions are equivalent:\n\n(i) Any element of G belongs to a subgroup H \\in X.\n\n(ii) The character of any irreducible representation of G belongs to the \\mathbb{Q}-span of characters of induced representations \\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V, where H \\in X and V is an irreducible representation of H.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem\u0009Artin's theorem: equivalence of element coverage and character span","header":"Artin's theorem: equivalence of element coverage and character span","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span/#Chapter5___Theorem5___26___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Proof-of-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___3":{"contents":"Proof of Burnside's theorem. Assume Burnside's theorem is false. Then there exists a nonsolvable group G of order p^a q^b. Let G be the smallest such group. Then G is simple, and by Theorem 5.4.6, it cannot have a conjugacy class of order p^k or q^k, k \\geq 1. So the order of any conjugacy class in G either equals 1 or is divisible by pq. Adding the orders of conjugacy classes and equating the sum to p^a q^b, we see that there has to be more than one conjugacy class consisting just of one element. So G has a nontrivial center, which gives a contradiction. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Proof of Burnside's theorem","header":"Proof of Burnside's theorem","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Proof-of-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___3"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/Problem-6___9___3___-Ext-and-Jordan-Holder-for-Dynkin-quiver-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___9___-Problems--Problem-6___9___3___-Ext-and-Jordan-Holder-for-Dynkin-quiver-representations--Problem-6___9___3___-Ext-and-Jordan-Holder-for-Dynkin-quiver-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"At a vertex satisfying the second distinguished property, every value of the displayed\nquiver-indexed type is related to its associated auxiliary object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAt a vertex satisfying the first distinguished property, its associated auxiliary object is\nrelated to every value of the displayed quiver-indexed type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nUnder the displayed ordering and finite-basis hypotheses, there is auxiliary data satisfying the two stated equalities.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Section6.9_heading; role=supporting]\n\nUnder the stated compatibility and thinness assumptions, there is a witness whose associated natural-number values agree with the supplied integer vector, as do those of a further witness.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nA designated elementary-extension relation between two subrepresentations at a vertex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nData for a finite chain of subrepresentations whose successive steps are indexed by vertices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]The number of steps in the vertex composition series.The subrepresentation at a natural-number stage of the composition series.The vertex associated with a step of the composition series.The initial step of the series is the bottom subrepresentation.The step at the length of the series is the top subrepresentation.Each consecutive pair of steps forms an elementary extension at its associated vertex.\n\nFor a finite vertex ordering that decreases along arrows and chosen vertex-space bases, there exists a series whose length is the sum of dimensions and whose vertex multiplicities are those dimensions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.9. Problems\u0009Problem 6.9.3: Ext and Jordan-Holder for Dynkin quiver representations\u0009Problem 6.9.3: Ext and Jordan-Holder for Dynkin quiver representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/Problem-6___9___3___-Ext-and-Jordan-Holder-for-Dynkin-quiver-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___9___-Problems--Problem-6___9___3___-Ext-and-Jordan-Holder-for-Dynkin-quiver-representations--Problem-6___9___3___-Ext-and-Jordan-Holder-for-Dynkin-quiver-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});